(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-micro-exam-1-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Microbiology Exam 1 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which resistant organism was cultured from peripheral computer devices?","choices":["MRSA","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Plasmodium falciparum","Clostridioides difficile"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, alongside coliforms. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is particularly effective at AGGLUTINATION, and why?","choices":["IgM — ten binding sites","IgG, because it is the most prevalent","IgE, because it binds mast cells","IgD, because it sits on B cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — cross-linking adjacent bacterial cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which statement about bacterial plasmids is correct?","choices":["They are the sole site of the bacterial chromosome","Small circular double-stranded DNA, free or integrated, which may encode antibiotic resistance, tolerance to toxic metals, enzymes and toxins","They are linear molecules, always integrated into the chromosome, and are essential to bacterial growth, to normal metabolism and to cell wall synthesis","They are protein structures used for motility"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They are duplicated and passed to offspring, and are readily manipulated for genetic engineering. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What makes the SECONDARY response faster and stronger?","choices":["Memory cells","A larger dose of antigen","Innate immunity taking over","Complement being activated sooner"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They produce a much higher titre of IgG, followed by gradual IgM. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Quinolones and fluoroquinolones act by which mechanism, and what are they used for?","choices":["Inhibiting RNA polymerase; used against tuberculosis","Inhibiting folic acid synthesis; used broadly","Binding the penicillin-binding proteins on the cell membrane; used mainly against Gram-positive organisms and some anaerobes","Inhibiting DNA gyrase, preventing DNA from unwinding; used for urinary tract infections and hospital acquired infections"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nalidixic acid is first generation; ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin and moxifloxacin are later generations. They are artificially produced. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is selective toxicity, and how is it normally achieved?","choices":["Selecting the narrowest possible spectrum for a given infection","Choosing a drug the organism has never been exposed to","Killing every microbe present in the sample; normally achieved by raising the dose above the upper limit of the therapeutic window until no organism survives","Working against the target pathogen without harming the host; normally achieved by interfering with processes or structures found in the pathogen's cells and not the host's"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is why drugs against eukaryotic pathogens are harder to find. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What health implications follow from high mutation rates in viruses?","choices":["Viruses lose the ability to replicate in host cells","Viruses become uniformly less transmissible over time","Viruses can no longer be identified by nucleic acid sequencing, and previously host-specific animal viruses lose the ability to cross to humans","Changing surface properties may weaken or eliminate immunity from vaccination or previous exposure, and animal viruses that were host specific can cross to humans"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Human hosts are unlikely to have immunity to viruses crossed from animal hosts. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Who coined the term “antibiotics”, and from which genus did his discoveries come?","choices":["Paul Ehrlich; Treponema","Gerhard Domagk; Penicillium","Alexander Fleming; Penicillium","Selman Waksman; Streptomyces"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Waksman discovered over twenty antibiotics from Streptomyces species and won the Nobel prize in 1952. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What do exoenzymes do?","choices":["Kill leukocytes directly","Prevent phagocytosis","Bind tightly to the surface of the host cell","Attack host defences to allow deeper invasion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — mucinase, hyaluronidase, coagulase, kinases. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How many links does the chain of infection have?","choices":["Four","Eight","Six","Three"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry, susceptible host. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How do antisense agents work, and what example is given?","choices":["They bind bacterial ribosomes to halt translation; example, nisin","They deliver bacteriophages to lyse the pathogen; example, phage therapy","They are complementary DNA that binds a pathogen's virulence genes and prevents transcription; example, fomivirsen for cytomegalovirus retinitis","They sequester the iron that a pathogen requires for its metabolism; example, diiodohydroxyquinoline used in amoebic disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Targeting virulence genes rather than the organism itself is what makes the approach distinctive. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What does the T SUPPRESSOR cell do?","choices":["Limits other T and B cells","Destroys virally infected cells","Coordinates the whole response","Mediates delayed allergy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What distinguishes a synthetic (chemically defined) medium from a complex one?","choices":["Synthetic inhibits unwanted organisms; complex encourages them","Synthetic shows visible differences between organisms; complex does not","Synthetic contains at least one ingredient that is not chemically definable; complex contains pure organic and inorganic compounds in an exact chemical formula","Synthetic contains pure organic and inorganic compounds in an exact chemical formula; complex contains at least one ingredient that is not chemically definable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is the whole distinction — whether every ingredient can be written as a formula. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is drug clearance?","choices":["Renal clearance plus hepatic clearance plus clearance from all other tissues","The proportion of an administered dose that reaches the systemic circulation intact","The concentration at which 99.9% of organisms are killed","The time taken for plasma concentration to fall by half"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That total is what determines how quickly concentration falls, and therefore the dosage schedule. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Bactericidal and bacteriostatic drugs are distinguished by what, and measured by which value respectively?","choices":["Bactericidal inhibits growth, measured by the minimal bactericidal concentration (the level inhibiting 99.9% of test organisms); bacteriostatic kills organisms directly, measured by the minimal inhibitory concentration","Both kill organisms; the difference is only in the speed of killing","Bactericidal works on Gram-positives, bacteriostatic on Gram-negatives","Bactericidal kills organisms directly, measured by minimal bactericidal concentration (the level killing 99.9% of test organisms); bacteriostatic inhibits growth, measured by minimal inhibitory concentration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Note the precise definition of minimal bactericidal concentration as the level killing 99.9%. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What distinguishes an epidemic from a pandemic?","choices":["A pandemic is global","An epidemic is global","A pandemic affects only animals","An epidemic lasts longer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; an epidemic exceeds expectation in a population. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which grouping of microbe types is correct?","choices":["All listed microbes are eukaryotic except viruses","Bacteria are prokaryotic; fungi, protozoa and helminths are eukaryotic; viruses are noncellular infectious agents","Bacteria and viruses are prokaryotic; all others are eukaryotic","Bacteria and fungi are prokaryotic; protozoa and helminths are eukaryotic; viruses are simply very small bacteria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That is how the types of microbes divide. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which of these is a BENEFICIAL effect of fever?","choices":["It increases production and activity of neutrophils","It increases viral replication","It suppresses T cell proliferation","It reduces tissue resistance to tumour necrosis factor alpha"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside enhanced T cell proliferation and immune signalling. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"The pilus is coded by what, and used for what?","choices":["The chromosome; used for adhesion to mucosal surfaces","A plasmid; used in bacterial conjugation","A prophage; used in transduction","The spore coat; used in survival"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both halves are given explicitly, which is what links the pilus to horizontal gene transfer. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are the two roles of C5a?","choices":["It recruits neutrophils","It opsonises pathogens and forms pores","It inhibits complement and clears debris","It initiates the membrane attack complex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — chemotaxis plus inflammation. It is the most potent anaphylatoxin. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is true of the urinary tract in females?","choices":["Sterile except a short portion of the urethra","Sterile throughout, including the urethra","Colonised throughout by lactobacilli","Colonised only after catheterisation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the same is true in males. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Minimal bactericidal concentration is defined as the minimum level that does what?","choices":["Kills 99.9% of test organisms","Inhibits the growth of an organism","Reaches the target site in the body","Produces a measurable zone of inhibition on disk diffusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Minimal inhibitory concentration, by contrast, is the minimum level that inhibits growth. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does HORIZONTAL transmission usually involve?","choices":["Mucous membrane contact","Mother to child","Contaminated drinking water","A mosquito bite"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Disease may be caused by factors other than the microbe itself. Which?","choices":["Antibiotic resistance genes","The infectious dose","The portal of exit","Our own immune responses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; disease may follow from the host response as much as the organism. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which site carries the highest numbers of bacteria?","choices":["Upper respiratory tract","Large intestine","Skin","Oral cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, mainly strict or facultative anaerobes. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What happens if natural killer cells cannot contain an infection?","choices":["They call in dendritic cells","The infection is cleared by complement alone","Nothing further happens","The macrophage takes over killing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and control passes to T cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin lines the mucosa and appears in saliva, tears, colostrum and mucus?","choices":["IgM","IgE","IgA","IgG"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — second most prevalent, conferring local immunity against enteric, respiratory and genitourinary pathogens. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the difference between an ANTI-FOULING and an ANTIMICROBIAL coating?","choices":["Anti-fouling kills; antimicrobial prevents attachment","Anti-fouling is used only on skin","Anti-fouling prevents attachment; antimicrobial kills","They are two words for the same thing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which of these is named as a soil-related route of transmission?","choices":["Crowded indoor spaces","Buffets","Poorly washed produce","Swimming pools"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — with hands and fingernails, and pinworms in children. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How are hospital-acquired infections PRIMARILY transmitted?","choices":["By insect vectors","Through contaminated soil","Directly, human to human","Indirectly, by fomite contact"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — fomite contact is the secondary route. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"By how much do C-reactive protein and serum amyloid A rise?","choices":["About tenfold","They fall rather than rise","Over a hundredfold","About twofold"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is why C-reactive protein is used diagnostically. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What are the reservoirs of influenza?","choices":["Birds and pigs","Mammals by bite","Fruit bats","Contaminated soil"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, transmitted by respiratory droplet and contact. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the practical consequence of drug clearance for the dosage schedule?","choices":["Clearance affects only the loading dose, not the interval","You must not let the concentration fall below the minimal inhibitory concentration — and it takes time for the drug to enter the system in the first place","Clearance determines the spectrum of activity","Doses should be spaced as widely apart as the patient will tolerate, since the drug persists in tissue long after the plasma level falls"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both halves matter: the trough must stay above the minimal inhibitory concentration, and onset is not instantaneous. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Remdesivir is described as which kind of agent, acting how?","choices":["A protease inhibitor blocking cleavage of viral polypeptide precursors","An integrase inhibitor preventing viral genes from entering the host chromosome after reverse transcription","A neuraminidase inhibitor preventing virus escape from the host cell","A nucleotide analog that binds viral RNA-dependent RNA polymerase and terminates RNA transcription prematurely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It has broad-spectrum activity against Ebola, respiratory syncytial virus and coronaviruses. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a cytopathic effect of viruses?","choices":["Conversion of a trophozoite to a cyst","Cells fusing to form multinucleated cells","Formation of an endospore coat","Crosslinking of peptidoglycan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The full list also includes changes in size and shape, cytoplasmic and nuclear inclusion bodies, cell lysis, alteration of deoxyribonucleic acid, and transformation into cancerous cells. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What distinguishes a superinfection from an ordinary secondary infection?","choices":["It occurs only in hospital","It is the first infection in the sequence of events","It follows disruption of the natural microflora","It always involves a virus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, classically after antibiotic therapy. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which interleukin does the APC secrete, and what else does it do?","choices":["Interleukin 2 — it is an endogenous pyrogen","Interferon gamma — it activates macrophages","Interleukin 6 — it drives the acute-phase response","Interleukin 1"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It activates the T helper cell. It is an endogenous pyrogen that stimulates fever and inflammation. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which ending indicates a CHIMERIC monoclonal antibody?","choices":["-zumab","-omab","-umab","-ximab"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which of these is normally sterile?","choices":["The lumen of the large intestine","The skin surface and hair follicles","The oral cavity and pharynx","Blood and cerebrospinal fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside kidneys, bladder, brain and the interior of the eye. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which line of defense is the SPECIFIC one, and what does it create?","choices":["The second — it creates inflammation","All three are specific","The third — it creates immunological memory","The first — it creates a physical seal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acquired on exposure to antigen, producing antibodies or defensive cell lines. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What must an antigen-presenting cell use to bring bound antigen to a T helper cell?","choices":["A complement receptor","A class II MHC receptor","A class I MHC receptor","An immunoglobulin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is required for the APC to activate the T helper cell. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which pairing of antihelminthic drug to mechanism is correct?","choices":["Mebendazole paralyzes the worm; ivermectin inhibits nutrient absorption","Niclosamide prevents ATP generation (tapeworms); praziquantel alters membrane permeability (flatworms)","All four act by inhibiting microtubule formation","Praziquantel prevents ATP generation (tapeworms); niclosamide alters membrane permeability (flatworms)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mebendazole inhibits nutrient absorption and ivermectin paralyzes the worm, both for intestinal roundworms. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What distinguishes generalized from specialized transduction?","choices":["Generalized occurs in the lysogenic cycle; specialized in the lytic","Generalized transduction transfers plasmid DNA cell to cell; specialized takes up naked DNA","Generalized requires a pilus; specialized requires a capsule","Generalized transmits random pieces of host DNA; specialized means all cells carry the same host DNA"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Generalized is listed under the lytic cycle, specialized under the lysogenic. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which cytokine dilates blood vessels to produce heat, swelling, redness and pain?","choices":["CXCL8","Interferon gamma","Tumour necrosis factor","Interleukin 6"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — increasing blood volume in the infected area. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What makes up the FIRST line of defense?","choices":["Complement and interferon alone","Barriers at the portal of entry","Protective cells and antimicrobial substances","Antibodies and defensive cell lines"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and all of it is nonspecific — it blocks invasion before entry. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the reservoir of rabies?","choices":["Fruit bats","Soil","Mammals","Birds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, transmitted by bite and saliva. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"In a healthy individual with robust immune responses, what happens to antibody titre over time?","choices":["It rises indefinitely","It is undetectable after the primary response","It typically does NOT fall to zero","It returns to zero between exposures"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which mechanism requires a bacteriophage acting as a vector?","choices":["Binary fission","Transduction","Conjugation","Transformation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Why is C-reactive protein used in diagnosis?","choices":["It falls predictably in infection","It identifies the organism responsible","It rises over a hundredfold","It is specific to bacterial infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a large, measurable swing. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is an opsonin?","choices":["An enzyme that cleaves peptidoglycan","A protein that promotes phagocytosis","A pore-forming protein","A cytokine that raises temperature"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — C3b is the example. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which are examples of vectors?","choices":["Airborne droplet nuclei","Mosquitoes, fleas and ticks","Soil and water sources","Fomites and vehicles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with flies and cockroaches also named. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What do all viruses have, and what shapes does it take?","choices":["Both deoxyribonucleic acid and ribonucleic acid together","Capsids — protein coats enclosing and protecting the nucleic acid — which may be helical, icosahedral or complex","Envelopes — lipid coats enclosing and protecting the capsid — which may be helical, icosahedral or complex","Cell walls of peptidoglycan, which may be thick or thin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Viruses contain either deoxyribonucleic acid or ribonucleic acid, and may additionally have an envelope or spikes. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What percentage of white cells are NEUTROPHILS, and what is their role?","choices":["55 to 90 per cent; they are phagocytes","1 to 3 per cent; they are phagocytes","20 to 35 per cent; they are phagocytes","0.5 per cent; they release chemical mediators"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — lobed nuclei with lavender granules. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Viral alteration of host deoxyribonucleic acid may activate oncogenes. What else can do this?","choices":["Exposure to radiation or chemicals","Conjugation and transformation","Desiccation and phagocytosis","Osmotic pressure and pH change"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Radiation and chemicals activate oncogenes alongside viruses, which is what links infection to malignant transformation. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How do the requirements for bacterial growth divide?","choices":["Synthetic versus complex","Chemical requirements — water, energy nutrients, vitamins, minerals, oxygen levels — and physical requirements — temperature, pH, osmotic pressure","Aerobic versus anaerobic oxygen requirements only — with temperature, pH and osmotic pressure counted as chemical rather than physical","Selective versus differential"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Energy nutrients are given as carbohydrates, proteins and lipids. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What explains the presence of more than a hundred human genes of microbial origin?","choices":["Random mutation","Laboratory contamination","Horizontal gene transfer","Convergent evolution"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, from bacteria, fungi and plants over time. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In diseases such as plague and anthrax, what role do humans usually play?","choices":["The vector","The accidental host","The primary host","The definitive host"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lifecycle runs through primary and secondary hosts. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What distinguishes mortality from morbidity rate?","choices":["Mortality counts cases, morbidity counts deaths","Mortality is a percentage, morbidity is absolute","They measure the same thing","Mortality counts deaths, morbidity counts cases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, both expressed per hundred thousand. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What does an ACTIVATED T helper cell produce, and what is its standing in the immune system?","choices":["Interleukin 1 — and it is the boss","Antibody — and it is the boss","Perforins — and it is the boss","Interleukin 2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It assists the other immune cells and coordinates the response. And it is described as the boss of the immune system. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What happens to lymphocyte clones that develop a specificity for SELF?","choices":["They migrate to the thymus to mature","They are converted into plasma cells","They are eliminated in the fetus","They are retained as memory cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A defect at this point gives an inherited autoimmune disorder such as severe combined immunodeficiency. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 17"}]};
